$ man gcc 2>/dev/null | col -b | grep -n -A6 -E '^ +-Wall$|^ +-Wextra$|^ +-Werror$|^ +-Wpedantic$|^ +-pedantic-errors$' | head -80
5097:     -Werror
5098-	 Turn all warnings into errors.
5099-
5100-     -Werror=
5101-	 Turn the specified warning into an error.  The specifier for a warning
5102-	 is appended; for example -Werror=switch turns the warnings controlled
5103-	 by -Wswitch into errors.  This switch takes a negative form, to be
--
5155:     -Wpedantic
5156-     -pedantic
5157-     --pedantic
5158-	 Issue all the warnings demanded by strict ISO C and ISO C++; diagnose
5159-	 all programs that use forbidden extensions, and some other programs
5160-	 that do not follow ISO C and ISO C++.	This follows the version of the
5161-	 ISO C or C++ standard specified by any -std option used.
--
5192:     -pedantic-errors
5193-     --pedantic-errors
5194-	 Give an error whenever the base standard (see -Wpedantic) requires a
5195-	 diagnostic, in some cases where there is undefined behavior at
5196-	 compile-time and in some other cases that do not prevent compilation
5197-	 of programs that are valid according to the standard. This is not
5198-	 equivalent to -Werror=pedantic: the latter option is unlikely to be
--
5245:     -Wall
5246-     --all-warnings
5247-	 This enables all the warnings about constructions that some users
5248-	 consider questionable, and that are easy to avoid (or modify to
5249-	 prevent the warning), even in conjunction with macros.	 This also
5250-	 enables some language-specific warnings described in C++ Dialect
5251-	 Options and Objective-C and Objective-C++ Dialect Options.
--
5300:     -Wextra
5301-     --extra-warnings
5302-	 This enables some extra warning flags that are not enabled by -Wall.
5303-	 (This option used to be called -W.  The older name is still supported,
5304-	 but the newer name is more descriptive.)
5305-
5306-	 -Wabsolute-value (only for C/ObjC) -Walloc-size
[exit 0]

